  ],
  "text": "Your stomach lining is five days old. Your skeleton is about ten years old. You feel like one continuous thing. The material underneath is not. The lining of your gut sits in acid, so it is stripped and replaced within a week. Your skin rebuilds in about a month, and you leave the old one everywhere you go. A red blood cell survives four months of being squeezed through your vessels, then is retired. Bone looks permanent, but cells quietly dismantle and rebuild it over about ten years. A few things do stay. Some brain neurons and the lens of your eye are as old as you are. So you are not a set of parts being kept. You are a pattern being maintained. Almost nothing you are made of today was you a decade ago. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
